{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-5966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/assets/dd286aa5384dae22d8fff4bb29799078547064926697d71b0b0ca8739641408f.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-5966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-5966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f172aabb550ef588f316a9e67cd90d26fe285f855d7b406023ebb18e2110b778"} {"question" "q-5966" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-5966" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-5966" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/assets/d1318686a66856057fb04027ac52ff20266e628a498458b283ee93df1e4294eb.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y={e}^{-|x|}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand ${e}^{-|x|}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f172aabb550ef588f316a9e67cd90d26fe285f855d7b406023ebb18e2110b778"} {"question" "q-20701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=2+{x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{4}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2+0}-\\frac{1}{2+{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f172aabb550ef588f316a9e67cd90d26fe285f855d7b406023ebb18e2110b778"} {"question" "q-20789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-20789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-347615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-347615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-347615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f172aabb550ef588f316a9e67cd90d26fe285f855d7b406023ebb18e2110b778"} {"question" "q-347615" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-347615" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-351605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ and the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-351605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/assets/2b7b85dd8894c73275816a8ee08a8c8a41fb41a327d20ed7bedc489f7e2e55ff.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{3}))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-351605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f172aabb550ef588f316a9e67cd90d26fe285f855d7b406023ebb18e2110b778"} {"question" "q-351605" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"} {"question" "q-351605" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056069/state.json" "file_sha256" "a7fda2b24e37d3cee32d6d3a91061f107b058450647faf8bdef0f14fe311fc43"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1834 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
